Begin Exploring Related Columns
Relevant columns and their potential usage  

player  
• player_id: join key to identify the bowler.  
• player_name: to show the bowler’s name in the final output.  

ball_by_ball  
• match_id: joins all “per‑ball” tables and lets us isolate one match.  
• over_id / ball_id: allow us to aggregate runs at “per‑over” level.  
• bowler: the bowler who delivered the ball; used to attribute runs conceded.  
• innings_no: needed because every match has two innings and over numbers repeat.  

batsman_scored  
• match_id / over_id / ball_id / innings_no: join keys to ball_by_ball.  
• runs_scored: runs off the bat that count against the bowler.  

extra_runs  
• match_id / over_id / ball_id / innings_no: same join keys.  
• extra_runs: wides, no‑balls, byes, etc. – also charged to the bowler (except byes/leg‑byes in real cricket, but we keep them for this exercise unless told otherwise).  

match  
• match_id: lets us bring back high‑level info (season, venue …).  

Conditions that will be handy  
• GROUP BY match_id, innings_no, over_id, bowler to total runs in each over.  
• HAVING SUM(total_runs) = MAX(total_runs) OVER (PARTITION BY match_id) to isolate the “worst” over(s) of each match.  
• ORDER BY over_runs DESC LIMIT 3 to keep the top 3 across all matches.  

Below are 10 exploratory SQL queries, starting simple and ending with a full answer query.  
Each query has a short description and returns at most 20 rows.

```sql
-- 1) List distinct bowler IDs we have on record
SELECT DISTINCT bowler
FROM ball_by_ball
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 2) Map a few bowler IDs to names
SELECT DISTINCT p.player_id, p.player_name
FROM player AS p
JOIN ball_by_ball AS b
  ON p.player_id = b.bowler
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 3) Peek at runs off the bat (first 20 rows)
SELECT *
FROM batsman_scored
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 4) Peek at extra runs (first 20 rows)
SELECT *
FROM extra_runs
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 5) Join one ball to show bat + extra runs side‑by‑side
SELECT b.match_id,
       b.over_id,
       b.ball_id,
       b.innings_no,
       b.bowler,
       COALESCE(bs.runs_scored,0)  AS bat_runs,
       COALESCE(er.extra_runs ,0)  AS extra_runs
FROM ball_by_ball AS b
LEFT JOIN batsman_scored AS bs
       ON bs.match_id   = b.match_id
      AND bs.over_id    = b.over_id
      AND bs.ball_id    = b.ball_id
      AND bs.innings_no = b.innings_no
LEFT JOIN extra_runs AS er
       ON er.match_id   = b.match_id
      AND er.over_id    = b.over_id
      AND er.ball_id    = b.ball_id
      AND er.innings_no = b.innings_no
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 6) Compute total runs per over for one sample match (335987)
SELECT b.match_id,
       b.innings_no,
       b.over_id,
       b.bowler,
       SUM(COALESCE(bs.runs_scored,0) + COALESCE(er.extra_runs,0)) AS over_runs
FROM ball_by_ball AS b
LEFT JOIN batsman_scored AS bs
       ON bs.match_id   = b.match_id
      AND bs.over_id    = b.over_id
      AND bs.ball_id    = b.ball_id
      AND bs.innings_no = b.innings_no
LEFT JOIN extra_runs AS er
       ON er.match_id   = b.match_id
      AND er.over_id    = b.over_id
      AND er.ball_id    = b.ball_id
      AND er.innings_no = b.innings_no
WHERE b.match_id = 335987
GROUP BY b.match_id, b.innings_no, b.over_id, b.bowler
ORDER BY over_runs DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 7) Find the maximum runs conceded in any over of match 335987
